  "contentPlaintext": "<bold>ADR-001: Selection of Primary Relational Database Engine</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Status:</textStyle></bold> Approved\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Context Date:</textStyle></bold> May 28, 2026\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Document Version:</textStyle></bold> 1.0.0-Baseline\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Deciding Authors:</textStyle></bold> [Insert Tech Lead Name]\n<bold>1. Table of Contents</bold>\n1. Table of Contents\n2. Architectural Context & Problem Statement\n3. Evaluated Technical Alternatives\n4. Decision Outcome & Strategic Rationale\n5. Consequences & Operational Impact\n<bold>2. Architectural Context & Problem Statement</bold>\n<bold>2.1 Technical Drivers</bold>\nOur platform requires a primary persistence layer to handle structured transactional data arrays. The system must guarantee complete write reliability under high concurrency volumes while keeping P95 query response speeds under 200 milliseconds.\n<bold>2.2 System Constraints</bold>\nThe choice of database must satisfy the following strict operational boundaries:\nMaintain strict data consistency for financial records and account matrices.\nSupport native multi-tenant data isolation frameworks easily.\nOperate efficiently within constrained cloud container environments (2 CPU / 4GB RAM limits).\n<bold>3. Evaluated Technical Alternatives</bold>\n<bold>3.1 Alternative A: PostgreSQL (Relational DBMS)</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Description:</textStyle></bold> A mature, open-source object-relational database system emphasizing compliance and extensibility.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Advantages:</textStyle></bold> Complete support for complex data normalization, strong indexing capabilities, and stable connection scaling modules.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Disadvantages:</textStyle></bold> Requires careful memory tuning to maintain low resource footprints during high connection concurrency spikes.\n<bold>3.2 Alternative B: MongoDB (Document-Oriented NoSQL)</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Description:</textStyle></bold> A distributed, schema-less document store utilizing JSON-like BSON models.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Advantages:</textStyle></bold> High horizontal scale metrics and quick ingestion speeds for variable unstructured payload files.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Disadvantages:</textStyle></bold> Lacks strict foreign key structural constraints at the storage layer, increasing data corruption risks across related multi-tenant tables.\n<bold>4. Decision Outcome & Strategic Rationale</bold>\n<bold>4.1 Selected Alternative</bold>\nThe team selected <bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Alternative A: PostgreSQL</textStyle></bold> as the primary datastore engine for our core system platform architecture.\n<bold>4.2 Rationale Matrix</bold>\nPostgreSQL provides the precise consistency capabilities required to safeguard relational matrices. Its built-in row-level locking mechanisms and indexing tools ensure our application can process concurrent tenant operations safely without risking state contamination. NoSQL alternatives fail to deliver the strict operational constraints needed for our core tracking data modules.\n<bold>5. Consequences & Operational Impact</bold>\n<bold>5.1 Positive Impacts</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Data Safety:</textStyle></bold> Strict relational schemas guarantee all data commits conform to target definitions automatically.\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Query Speeds:</textStyle></bold> Foreign key indexes keep complex lookups running inside our targeted sub-300ms limits.\n<bold>5.2 Negative Impacts & Mitigations</bold>\n<bold><textStyle color=\"\" fontFamily=\"\" fontSize=\"null\">Connection Overhead:</textStyle></bold> PostgreSQL spawns process threads per client channel, which can exhaust container memory.\n<italic>Mitigation:</italic> The Engineering team will deploy a dedicated connection pooling utility ahead of the datastore instance to reuse open connections and preserve container memory limits.",
